\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The positive-definiteness condition, drawn. (a) Because $A$ has constant diagonal $s$ and $0/1$ off-diagonals, every Gershgorin disc is centred at $s$ with radius equal to that segment's number of compatible neighbours $d_i$; the sufficient condition $\gamma +\delta >d_{\qopname  \relax m{max}}$ is exactly the statement that no disc reaches the origin. (b) Why the condition is so much weaker than the truth: the degree distribution is overwhelmingly concentrated at $d_i=0$ --- $98.6\%$ even under heavy noise --- and only eleven segments in $157{,}609$ reach $d_i=s$. (c) The control knob. Equation\nobreakspace  {}\textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:gammaknob}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} makes $\lambda _{\qopname  \relax m{min}}(A)$ a unit-slope line in $\gamma $, so the self-penalty needed for positive definiteness can be read off exactly (triangles); $\gamma =3$ clears it at every configuration used here.\relax }}{8}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gershgorin}{{2}{8}{The positive-definiteness condition, drawn. (a) Because $A$ has constant diagonal $s$ and $0/1$ off-diagonals, every Gershgorin disc is centred at $s$ with radius equal to that segment's number of compatible neighbours $d_i$; the sufficient condition $\gamma +\delta >d_{\max }$ is exactly the statement that no disc reaches the origin. (b) Why the condition is so much weaker than the truth: the degree distribution is overwhelmingly concentrated at $d_i=0$ --- $98.6\%$ even under heavy noise --- and only eleven segments in $157{,}609$ reach $d_i=s$. (c) The control knob. Equation~\eqref {eq:gammaknob} makes $\lambda _{\min }(A)$ a unit-slope line in $\gamma $, so the self-penalty needed for positive definiteness can be read off exactly (triangles); $\gamma =3$ clears it at every configuration used here.\relax }{figure.caption.3}{}}
\newlabel{eq:dpform}{{6}{8}{The Hamiltonian}{equation.2.6}{}}
\newlabel{eq:epsilon}{{7}{9}{The Hamiltonian}{equation.2.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Where the angular acceptance comes from. (a) The geometry of one measured kink: the particle takes a scattering kick in each projection at the shared plane, and all three hits are recorded with an independent position error, so the measured kink is the second difference of three smeared coordinates (schematic; the true angles are a few milliradians and are drawn hugely exaggerated). (b) The resulting three-dimensional kink is Rayleigh-distributed, not Gaussian: the histogram is $3600$ true kinks measured on six generated events at the heavy configuration ($\sigma _{\rm  scatt}=10^{-4}$, $\sigma _{\rm  res}=20\,\mu $m, $T=200$), the curve is Rayleigh($\sigma _p$) with $\sigma _p$ from the closed form and no fit, and the vertical is the acceptance eq.\nobreakspace  {}\textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:epsilon}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} sets, which by construction discards $e^{-9}=1.2\times 10^{-4}$ of true continuations.\relax }}{10}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:epsilon}{{3}{10}{Where the angular acceptance comes from. (a) The geometry of one measured kink: the particle takes a scattering kick in each projection at the shared plane, and all three hits are recorded with an independent position error, so the measured kink is the second difference of three smeared coordinates (schematic; the true angles are a few milliradians and are drawn hugely exaggerated). (b) The resulting three-dimensional kink is Rayleigh-distributed, not Gaussian: the histogram is $3600$ true kinks measured on six generated events at the heavy configuration ($\sigma _{\rm scatt}=10^{-4}$, $\sigma _{\rm res}=20\,\mu $m, $T=200$), the curve is Rayleigh($\sigma _p$) with $\sigma _p$ from the closed form and no fit, and the vertical is the acceptance eq.~\eqref {eq:epsilon} sets, which by construction discards $e^{-9}=1.2\times 10^{-4}$ of true continuations.\relax }{figure.caption.4}{}}
