\citation{trackhhl}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The amplitude atlas: exact activation level of every canonical cluster type (rows), classical (top) and after the 1BQF (bottom). This figure is analytic: it is the exact solution of $A\mathbf  {x}=\delta \mathbf  {1}$ on each cluster in isolation at $\gamma =3$, $\delta =1$, $s=4$, with no events, no noise and no acceptance; the filtered amplitudes are the exact readout of the one-bit filter circuit, eq.\nobreakspace  {}\textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:onebitreadout}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} of section\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:onebit}, placed on the classical scale by one global factor, so the two panels are directly comparable. The dashed line is the threshold that would keep every segment of a \emph  {complete} five-hit track, $4/11$ classically and $0.266$ after the filter: on clean events, where every true segment belongs to such a track, it is the efficiency-first threshold of the analytic picture. Two things are then visible that the rest of this work spends its time on. The true fragments of broken tracks fall \emph  {below} that line, on top of the false levels. And every cluster pair with the same graph carries exactly the same amplitudes in both panels: a true two-segment fragment sits on the false pair, a true three-segment fragment on the false bridge, and the one-segment stub sits on the isolated false segment at $\lambda =s$, so the filter's single zero annihilates the two together --- it removes every isolated fake, and every one-segment true fragment with them. Green: true; pink: false. Cluster names and their graphs are defined in section\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:toy:graphs}; a stub is a one-segment true fragment ($P_1$) and a hub is a star ($K_{1,m}$).\relax }}{15}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:atlas}{{6}{15}{The amplitude atlas: exact activation level of every canonical cluster type (rows), classical (top) and after the 1BQF (bottom). This figure is analytic: it is the exact solution of $A\mathbf {x}=\delta \mathbf {1}$ on each cluster in isolation at $\gamma =3$, $\delta =1$, $s=4$, with no events, no noise and no acceptance; the filtered amplitudes are the exact readout of the one-bit filter circuit, eq.~\eqref {eq:onebitreadout} of section~\ref {sec:onebit}, placed on the classical scale by one global factor, so the two panels are directly comparable. The dashed line is the threshold that would keep every segment of a \emph {complete} five-hit track, $4/11$ classically and $0.266$ after the filter: on clean events, where every true segment belongs to such a track, it is the efficiency-first threshold of the analytic picture. Two things are then visible that the rest of this work spends its time on. The true fragments of broken tracks fall \emph {below} that line, on top of the false levels. And every cluster pair with the same graph carries exactly the same amplitudes in both panels: a true two-segment fragment sits on the false pair, a true three-segment fragment on the false bridge, and the one-segment stub sits on the isolated false segment at $\lambda =s$, so the filter's single zero annihilates the two together --- it removes every isolated fake, and every one-segment true fragment with them. Green: true; pink: false. Cluster names and their graphs are defined in section~\ref {sec:toy:graphs}; a stub is a one-segment true fragment ($P_1$) and a hub is a star ($K_{1,m}$).\relax }{figure.caption.8}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.5}Which modes the bias can excite}{15}{subsection.2.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:toy:modes}{{2.5}{15}{Which modes the bias can excite}{subsection.2.5}{}}
