\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces What a true track's four eigenvalues actually carry. (a) The track and its graph, the chain $P_4$. (b--e) The four modes of that chain, drawn as their weight on each segment: every mode is spread over all four segments, so no mode belongs to a segment. (c) and (e) are antisymmetric under the chain's reflection, and the uniform bias $\mathbf  {b}=\delta \mathbf  {1}$ is symmetric, so their overlap with it vanishes identically: they are \emph  {dark}, carrying an eigenvalue but no amplitude. (f) The exact solution is therefore built from the two bright modes and nothing else, $98.5\%$ of its weight sitting on $\lambda =2.382$, and it reproduces $4/11$ and $5/11$ exactly.\relax }}{17}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:trackmodes}{{7}{17}{What a true track's four eigenvalues actually carry. (a) The track and its graph, the chain $P_4$. (b--e) The four modes of that chain, drawn as their weight on each segment: every mode is spread over all four segments, so no mode belongs to a segment. (c) and (e) are antisymmetric under the chain's reflection, and the uniform bias $\mathbf {b}=\delta \mathbf {1}$ is symmetric, so their overlap with it vanishes identically: they are \emph {dark}, carrying an eigenvalue but no amplitude. (f) The exact solution is therefore built from the two bright modes and nothing else, $98.5\%$ of its weight sitting on $\lambda =2.382$, and it reproduces $4/11$ and $5/11$ exactly.\relax }{figure.caption.9}{}}
