\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces The chain ladder, on the same analytic footing as figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:atlas} ($\gamma =3$, $\delta =1$, $s=4$, no noise; the 1BQF levels are the readout of eq.\nobreakspace  {}\textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:onebitreadout}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} on the classical scale by the atlas' single global factor). Classical outer activation climbs eq.\nobreakspace  {}\textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:ladder}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} toward $0.366$; the 1BQF pushes the outer level of a five-hit track down to $0.266$ and the middles up to $0.492$, and annihilates the one-segment stub outright, since a lone segment sits exactly on the notch. The marked levels are what a threshold has to clear to keep every segment of a complete track.\relax }}{26}{figure.caption.19}\protected@file@percent }
