\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces How the polynomial is found. (a) The comb target and its least-squares Chebyshev realisation at degrees $8$, $16$, $24$ and $40$ over the populated spectrum (dotted green: the true lines; dashed: the isolated line; dotted red: the pair lines), each normalised to its peak; the passes sharpen as the resolvable width $2W/d$ shrinks. The minimax fit at degree $16$ has the smaller worst-case error and spreads it over the whole domain. (b) The Chebyshev coefficients at degrees $16$ and $40$. (c) The one-norm $\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _k|c_k|$, the peak $\qopname  \relax m{max}|p|$, and their squared ratio, which is the success-probability penalty the one-norm realisation pays relative to the phase sequence: $2.2$ at $d=40$. (d) The resolution law $2W/d$ against the widths that matter: the pass, the closest true-line spacing, and the gap between the triple line and the nearest true line.\relax }}{33}{figure.caption.27}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:combdeg}{{17}{33}{How the polynomial is found. (a) The comb target and its least-squares Chebyshev realisation at degrees $8$, $16$, $24$ and $40$ over the populated spectrum (dotted green: the true lines; dashed: the isolated line; dotted red: the pair lines), each normalised to its peak; the passes sharpen as the resolvable width $2W/d$ shrinks. The minimax fit at degree $16$ has the smaller worst-case error and spreads it over the whole domain. (b) The Chebyshev coefficients at degrees $16$ and $40$. (c) The one-norm $\sum _k|c_k|$, the peak $\max |p|$, and their squared ratio, which is the success-probability penalty the one-norm realisation pays relative to the phase sequence: $2.2$ at $d=40$. (d) The resolution law $2W/d$ against the widths that matter: the pass, the closest true-line spacing, and the gap between the triple line and the nearest true line.\relax }{figure.caption.27}{}}
