\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29}{\ignorespaces Polynomial degree against the two segment metrics on moderate noise ($T{=}200$, ten repetitions; per-repetition dots, median lines; response refitted at every degree). Stars at the left edge: the matched 1BQF, a degree-one filter, on each operator. (a) Efficiency at a $1\%$ false-rate budget: every operator is flat from $d{\approx }8$ — the response axis is exhausted almost immediately, at $0.975$--$0.978$ on base $A$ against the classical $0.492$. (b) False rate at the matched $99\%$-efficiency point: base $A$ and the fork sit pinned at the $0.31$ twin flood at \emph  {every} degree, while the occupancy operators alone drop to the $10^{-2}$ scale — purity at high efficiency is bought by the operator, not the polynomial.\relax }}{52}{figure.caption.42}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:degmod}{{29}{52}{Polynomial degree against the two segment metrics on moderate noise ($T{=}200$, ten repetitions; per-repetition dots, median lines; response refitted at every degree). Stars at the left edge: the matched 1BQF, a degree-one filter, on each operator. (a) Efficiency at a $1\%$ false-rate budget: every operator is flat from $d{\approx }8$ — the response axis is exhausted almost immediately, at $0.975$--$0.978$ on base $A$ against the classical $0.492$. (b) False rate at the matched $99\%$-efficiency point: base $A$ and the fork sit pinned at the $0.31$ twin flood at \emph {every} degree, while the occupancy operators alone drop to the $10^{-2}$ scale — purity at high efficiency is bought by the operator, not the polynomial.\relax }{figure.caption.42}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30}{\ignorespaces The same scan on heavy noise. The efficiency curves are sigmoids in $\qopname  \relax o{log}d$ whose onset degree is set by the operator: the fork rises first ($0.91$ by $d{=}16$), base $A$ next ($d{\approx }20$), the occupancy operators last ($d\gtrsim 64$), because the co-hit term widens the spectral domain and a fixed degree loses resolution proportionally (the span wall of section\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:atscale:density}). The 1BQF stars sit at zero for every operator here.\relax }}{53}{figure.caption.43}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:degheavy}{{30}{53}{The same scan on heavy noise. The efficiency curves are sigmoids in $\log d$ whose onset degree is set by the operator: the fork rises first ($0.91$ by $d{=}16$), base $A$ next ($d{\approx }20$), the occupancy operators last ($d\gtrsim 64$), because the co-hit term widens the spectral domain and a fixed degree loses resolution proportionally (the span wall of section~\ref {sec:atscale:density}). The 1BQF stars sit at zero for every operator here.\relax }{figure.caption.43}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31}{\ignorespaces The marginal value of one more degree step (the derivative of the sigmoids of figures\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:degmod}--\ref  {fig:degheavy}). (a) Moderate: after occupancy's onset step ($+0.94$ from $d{=}4$ to $8$, off scale) every operator's marginal gain is consistent with zero at every degree. (b) Heavy: the marginal gain peaks at the operator's onset and decays to zero past it, with the non-monotone ripple of the fitted family visible as negative excursions.\relax }}{53}{figure.caption.44}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:degmarg}{{31}{53}{The marginal value of one more degree step (the derivative of the sigmoids of figures~\ref {fig:degmod}--\ref {fig:degheavy}). (a) Moderate: after occupancy's onset step ($+0.94$ from $d{=}4$ to $8$, off scale) every operator's marginal gain is consistent with zero at every degree. (b) Heavy: the marginal gain peaks at the operator's onset and decays to zero past it, with the non-monotone ripple of the fitted family visible as negative excursions.\relax }{figure.caption.44}{}}
