\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {35}{\ignorespaces The figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:clean2x2} pair at the formula acceptance for every series — the classical solver, the 1BQF, and the degree-40 QSVT with coefficients refit on base $A$, $A+$occupancy, $A+$bifurcation, and both — at clean, moderate and heavy noise (rows), $T=200$--$1000$, every solver at its efficiency-first working point. Clean is solved at every multiplicity on every Hamiltonian. Under noise, every curve converges to the false prevalence as the twin flood completes: past the wall the $99\%$-efficiency point stops being a working point at all. The classical solver, read at the same working point, sits at $31\%$--$92\%$ (moderate) and $63\%$--$94\%$ (heavy) false rate over this range.\relax }}{57}{figure.caption.48}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:density5}{{35}{57}{The figure~\ref {fig:clean2x2} pair at the formula acceptance for every series — the classical solver, the 1BQF, and the degree-40 QSVT with coefficients refit on base $A$, $A+$occupancy, $A+$bifurcation, and both — at clean, moderate and heavy noise (rows), $T=200$--$1000$, every solver at its efficiency-first working point. Clean is solved at every multiplicity on every Hamiltonian. Under noise, every curve converges to the false prevalence as the twin flood completes: past the wall the $99\%$-efficiency point stops being a working point at all. The classical solver, read at the same working point, sits at $31\%$--$92\%$ (moderate) and $63\%$--$94\%$ (heavy) false rate over this range.\relax }{figure.caption.48}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {36}{\ignorespaces The clean benchmark of figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:clean2x2} (fixed $\varepsilon =2$\,mrad, $\gamma =3$, no hit drop) with every solver at its efficiency-first working point ($99\%$ of true segments kept; panel (a) shows the convention holding): the classical solver, the 1BQF, and the QSVT (degree 40, base $A$, the clean-event line-comb coefficients; benchmark campaign, mean $\pm $ s.e.m.\ over 3--20 repetitions), together with the same degree-40 QSVT with coefficients refit on base $A$, $A+$occupancy ($\alpha =0.05$), $A+$bifurcation ($\beta =0.5$) and both (repetitions 0--2 of the same events). The line-comb coefficients stop winning once the $2$\,mrad events develop tangles ($T\ge 400$); the refit restores the margin, $2.8\%$ against the classical $10.0\%$ at $T=1000$, on every Hamiltonian. A measured $0\%$ false rate is drawn at the $10^{-3}$ floor.\relax }}{58}{figure.caption.49}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fig6first}{{36}{58}{The clean benchmark of figure~\ref {fig:clean2x2} (fixed $\varepsilon =2$\,mrad, $\gamma =3$, no hit drop) with every solver at its efficiency-first working point ($99\%$ of true segments kept; panel (a) shows the convention holding): the classical solver, the 1BQF, and the QSVT (degree 40, base $A$, the clean-event line-comb coefficients; benchmark campaign, mean $\pm $ s.e.m.\ over 3--20 repetitions), together with the same degree-40 QSVT with coefficients refit on base $A$, $A+$occupancy ($\alpha =0.05$), $A+$bifurcation ($\beta =0.5$) and both (repetitions 0--2 of the same events). The line-comb coefficients stop winning once the $2$\,mrad events develop tangles ($T\ge 400$); the refit restores the margin, $2.8\%$ against the classical $10.0\%$ at $T=1000$, on every Hamiltonian. A measured $0\%$ false rate is drawn at the $10^{-3}$ floor.\relax }{figure.caption.49}{}}
