\newlabel{sec:limits:readout}{{11.7}{63}{Success probability and readout}{subsection.11.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38}{\ignorespaces The readout budget, measured on the shared events at the production degree $d=40$. (a) Post-selection success probability per repetition: the measured comb points follow $0.44/T$; the 1BQF estimate $0.25/T$ is shown for comparison. (b) Total walk calls per solve after amplitude amplification, following the $d\sqrt  T$ scaling.\relax }}{64}{figure.caption.53}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:readout}{{38}{64}{The readout budget, measured on the shared events at the production degree $d=40$. (a) Post-selection success probability per repetition: the measured comb points follow $0.44/T$; the 1BQF estimate $0.25/T$ is shown for comparison. (b) Total walk calls per solve after amplitude amplification, following the $d\sqrt T$ scaling.\relax }{figure.caption.53}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {11.8}Classical simulability of the response family}{64}{subsection.11.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:limits:dequant}{{11.8}{64}{Classical simulability of the response family}{subsection.11.8}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {11.9}The toy's geometry, and what a real detector changes}{64}{subsection.11.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:limits:geometry}{{11.9}{64}{The toy's geometry, and what a real detector changes}{subsection.11.9}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {39}{\ignorespaces The real-geometry counterpart of the toy's amplitude pile-up: activation-amplitude spectra on real Run-3 VELO event geometry, for the classical solver, the 1BQF, and the redesigned band-plus-notch response. Pink: false segments; orange: true fragments ($m\le 2$); green: findable true segments ($m\ge 3$); the dotted line is each solver's efficiency-first threshold, set so that $99\%$ of the \emph  {findable} true segments (tracks with three or more true segments) survive, with the per-event range shaded; over all true segments the same rule is degenerate, because the $13\%$ of true segments in fragments of one or two segments sit at the isolated level inside the false bulk. The QSVT here is the degree-40 band-limited design on the base Hamiltonian, not the line comb: real tracks have variable length, so the five-hit lines do not apply. Variable track length smears the discrete toy levels into bands, and the fragment population sits inside the false bulk for every solver, the real-geometry form of the same efficiency ceiling.\relax }}{65}{figure.caption.54}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:run3}{{39}{65}{The real-geometry counterpart of the toy's amplitude pile-up: activation-amplitude spectra on real Run-3 VELO event geometry, for the classical solver, the 1BQF, and the redesigned band-plus-notch response. Pink: false segments; orange: true fragments ($m\le 2$); green: findable true segments ($m\ge 3$); the dotted line is each solver's efficiency-first threshold, set so that $99\%$ of the \emph {findable} true segments (tracks with three or more true segments) survive, with the per-event range shaded; over all true segments the same rule is degenerate, because the $13\%$ of true segments in fragments of one or two segments sit at the isolated level inside the false bulk. The QSVT here is the degree-40 band-limited design on the base Hamiltonian, not the line comb: real tracks have variable length, so the five-hit lines do not apply. Variable track length smears the discrete toy levels into bands, and the fragment population sits inside the false bulk for every solver, the real-geometry form of the same efficiency ceiling.\relax }{figure.caption.54}{}}
